{"id": "168110", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168110", "title": "Haines Junction", "text": "Haines Junction is a village in southwestern Yukon with a population of 613 (2016). It is a major administrative centre for the Champagne and Aishihik First Nations.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nFor around 2,000 years, the Southern Tutchone people had seasonal hunting and fishing camps in the area of present-day Haines Junction. The original name of the area was \"Dakwakada\", a Southern Tutchone word meaning \"high cache\". It was common for Tutchone people to use raised log caches to store food year-round or temporarily while they hunted and fished in an area.\nThe Haines Junction area was also important for trade between the coastal and interior peoples. It lies at the interior end of the Chilkat Pass, one of only three passes that allowed travel between the coast and the interior, which was used extensively for trade between the coastal Tlingit and Southern Tutchone people.\nHaines Junction was established in 1942 and 1943 during the construction of the Alaska Highway. In 1943, a second highway, the Haines Highway, was built to connect the Alaska Highway with the coastal town of Haines, Alaska, over the Chilkat Pass, hence the name of the community. Situated at the junction of these two highways, Haines Junction was a construction camp and a supply and service centre for the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers building the highway.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThere are two highways that intersect at Haines Junction:\nBy plane.\nHaines Junction Airport has no scheduled airline service.\nGo next.\nKluane National Park"}
{"id": "168153", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168153", "title": "Pulau Hatta", "text": "Pulau Hatta (Hatta Island) is part of the Banda Islands in Maluku province, Indonesia and has lovely beaches and healthy coral reefs.\nUnderstand.\nThe original name of the island is 'Rozengain' island. It was renamed 'Hatta', to commemorate the first Vice President, Mohammad Hatta, who was exiled in Banda Neira.\nThere are two villages in Hatta Island. Most of the accommodation is in the Old Village (on the Northwest side on the island). The Old Village doesn't have its own water supply so you are recommended to use water sparingly. \nGet in.\nThere is at least a boat every two days connecting Hatta Island and Neira Island. The boat leaves Hatta Island in early morning for Banda Neira, then returns from Neira Island to Hatta Island around 11AM-1PM.\nDo.\nHatta Island is gaining popularity as the best place for snorkeling and diving in Banda Islands. If you are not staying in the island, a day trip with chartered boat from Neira Island is around Rp1,000,000 - Rp1,300,000 (as of Oct 2018) \nJungle trekking: some of locals can offer a half day trip to see Kuskus Bear and other endemic birds on the island.\nDrink.\nIt is possible to get a big can / bottle of beer. Price is about Rp35,000 (as of Nov 2018)\nSleep.\nThe village in Hatta Island set a regulation for all the accommodation to offer full board package (breakfast-lunch-dinner included) at a fixed price of Rp250,000 per night per person (as of Oct 2018). Solo traveller may be charged 20% more. This is to avoid price war between the accommodation and to avoid tourists being ripped off. Some of the accommodation may try their luck to charge higher during peak season. It is recommended to check the current regulated price in Neira Island before you go there.\nSince accommodation is limited, it is recommended to book your accommodation before going to the island. \nConnect.\nThere isn't internet in the island. Only Telkomsel is operating in Hatta Island and Banda Islands (as of Oct 2018).\nElectricity is only running in the evening for about three to four hours in the evening. Expect no internet at the accommodation (as of Oct 2018). \nGo next.\nTake the public boat back to Neira Island in the morning. Note that the boat is not operating on Friday."}
