{"id": "168971", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168971", "title": "Reefton", "text": "Reefton is a small inland town beside the Inangahua River in the West Coast region of New Zealand. With a population of 1,000, it is the 4th biggest town on the West Coast.\nUnderstand.\nThe town got its name after veins of gold were found in a quartz reef in the 19th century. Gold mining and coal mining have been major industries in the area. The Reefton hydroelectric power station came into operation in 1888 and the town became the first in the Southern Hemisphere to have a public electricity supply.\nGet in.\nIt is 77 km (1 hr) north-east of Greymouth on State Highway 7, and also 77 km (1 hr) south-east of Westport.\nGet around.\nYou can walk through the town."}
{"id": "168976", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168976", "title": "Redfield (Arkansas)", "text": "Redfield is a small city in the Timberlands region of Southern Arkansas.\nUnderstand.\nRedfield is situated on the Union Pacific railroad midway between Little Rock and Pine Bluff, Arkansas. It is home to six properties listed on the National Register of Historic Places. The American Legion post is thoroughly organized, while the public library attains a rare excellence. In 2013, it was named \"Volunteer Community of the Year\" by the State of Arkansas.\nHistory.\nRedfield was founded in 1880 by James K. Brodie and named in honor of Jared E. Redfield of Essex, Connecticut; fifth president of the Little Rock, Mississippi River and Texas Railway. It was incorporated in 1898. It was partially burnt In 1903, but has been growing steadily since 1970.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest major airport is the Bill &amp; Hillary Clinton National Airport (), about 25 minutes away. The best way to get into downtown Redfield is by car.\nBy car.\nI-530 is how most motorists will enter the city. US-65 is another major road heading north\u2013south. Other notable main roads include Highway 46 and Highway 365.\nGet around.\nOutside of the downtown area (consisting roughly of a few blocks surrounding the Union Pacific Railroad), a car is definitely your best bet."}
{"id": "168986", "revid": "2428195", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168986", "title": "GR 21", "text": "The GR 21 is a coastal footpath in France, in the Grande Randonn\u00e9e network.\nUnderstand.\nThis 186 km route takes in the entire Alabaster Coast, its glorious chalk cliffs and snug harbour towns. It tracks north-east from Le Havre to Le Tr\u00e9port on the Norman/Picard border.\nPrepare.\nNo permits needed \u2014 just show up and walk. The clifftop paths and rocky coastal terrain mean this is strictly a backpack-only trail; wheeled luggage, strollers, and wheelchairs won't get far. Good hiking boots are a must (ankle support will save you on the rocky sections). After heavy rain or frost, some sections can be treacherous, so check conditions before heading out.\nEat.\nYou won't go hungry on the GR 21. The route hops between coastal towns and villages, and you can count on finding somewhere to eat or stock up pretty much every day. Leave the camping stove at home.\nDrink.\nWith towns never too far apart, most hikers just grab drinks at caf\u00e9s and restaurants along the way. There are also streams, springs, and the occasional well, though you'll want to filter anything from natural sources before drinking it.\nSleep.\nEach stage of the trail has hotels, guesthouses, or campsites to choose from, so you don't need to carry a tent unless you want to. Wild camping isn't allowed. In summer, book ahead \u2014 the popular spots fill up fast.\nClimate.\nNormandy weather is famously moody: sunny one moment, windy and wet the next, whatever the season. May through October is generally the most enjoyable time to walk, but the trail is doable year-round. Pack a windproof and waterproof layer no matter when you go, and don't forget sunscreen for the bright days on the open clifftops.\nGet in.\nThe trail begins in Le Havre.\nWalk.\nAfter leaving Le Havre, the trail ascends to Montivilliers. The next part of the route is along the cliffs of the Pays de Caux; a notable sight is \u00c9tretat, which is known for its chalk formations. The trail goes through many resorts and villages and eventually leads to Le Tr\u00e9port near Eu.\nThe trail begins at \u2014 literally \"The Harbour\", Le Havre is Paris's port town and a UNESCO World Heritage Site for its post-WWII modernist reconstruction \u2014 setting off from the Fort de Sainte-Adresse on the western edge of the city. From here the route heads east, quickly leaving the urban landscape behind as it enters the wooded For\u00eat de Montgeon, a popular local green space with small fruit trees (mostly blackberries) lining the waymarked paths. Continuing east through the Parc de Rouelles, the trail then swings north, passing through the town of Montivilliers before reaching the small village of. This stretch stays inland and doesn't yet reach the coast or the chalk cliffs the GR 21 is best known for further along the route \u2014 the scenery here is gentle countryside rather than seaside drama \u2014 but it makes for an easy, low-effort first leg with modest ascent. Accommodation options are sparse in Rolleville itself, so it's worth booking ahead if stopping overnight here.\nThis stretch continues through the rolling farmland of the Pays de Caux, passing near Notre-Dame-du-Bec, Turretot, and Criquetot-l'Esneval before turning toward the coast. The path here alternates between open fields and the edges of small woodlands, with charming Norman villages, manor houses, and old churches dotting the way \u2014 a peaceful, easy walk with little elevation change. As you near, a small farming village just southwest of \u00c9tretat, the smell of the sea starts to reach the trail at last. This is where the GR 21 truly meets the coast: from the beach at Le Tilleul, the path picks up the clifftop route that will follow the Alabaster Coast's chalk cliffs for the rest of the trail.\nThis is where the GR 21 comes into its own, following the clifftop path high above the Channel with a series of steep but short climbs in and out of the \"valleuses\" \u2014 the perched valleys that cut down through the chalk to the sea. The trail soon reaches \u2014 near many cliffs on the shoreline, this town inspired many of Claude Monet's paintings \u2014 where the arches of the Porte d'Aval and the Manneporte flank the needle-like rock of l'Aiguille, best admired from the clifftop Chapelle Notre-Dame de la Garde just south of town. From \u00c9tretat the path climbs again toward B\u00e9nouville and Les Loges before reaching Vattetot-sur-Mer, where the ascents and descents grow noticeably steeper and more exposed. The trail then descends a final time into, a small former fishing village tucked into a green valley, its pebble beach lined with brightly painted boats and the blue-and-white beach huts that have become the village's trademark.\nLeaving Yport, the trail dips through the wooded valleuses of Grainval and Vaucottes before climbing to \u2014 beach, port, and \"B\u00e9n\u00e9dictine Palace\" \u2014 a working fishing port whose museum, housed in a former cod-drying warehouse, tells the story of the town's long relationship with the sea. From the harbour, the GR 21 climbs steeply along the ancient Gallo-Roman C\u00f4te de la Vierge path to Cap Fagnet, the highest point on the Alabaster Coast at around 110 m, passing the small Notre-Dame-du-Salut chapel with its collection of sailors' votive offerings and a scattering of WWII-era blockhouses along the clifftop. From there the trail continues north above the cliffs, passing the beaches of Petites Dalles and Grandes Dalles, before descending into, a quiet clifftop village marking the end of the stage.\nThe trail continues east along the clifftops past bourgeois villas favoured by Normandy's Impressionist painters, before dropping inland through the fields of the Pays de Caux to reach Veulettes-sur-Mer. From there the path skirts the grounds of the nuclear power station, one of the largest in France, before rejoining the coast to finish at, a fishing port turned marina town. The half-timbered Maison Henri IV, now a small museum, and the town's WWII memorial recall the Battle of Saint-Valery of June 1940, when British and French troops were encircled here by Rommel's forces and much of the town centre was destroyed; it was rebuilt after the war and today its green-and-white lighthouse marks the harbour entrance.\nFrom Saint-Valery-en-Caux the trail follows the clifftops onward along the chalk cliffs, alternating between open farmland and short, steep dips into the valleuses, and descending into \u2014 the location of ancient water mills. The village grew up around the Veules, France's shortest river at just over a kilometre long, and a footpath along its banks passes thatched Norman cottages, watercress beds, and several of the eleven mills that once lined its course, grinding grain and fulling cloth using its remarkably steady flow. Veules-les-Roses is often ranked among France's most beautiful villages and makes a pleasant place to break the journey before continuing on toward Dieppe.\nLeaving Veules-les-Roses, the trail climbs back up onto the cliffs and settles into a rhythm of flower-flecked farmland and open sea views, with the flax fields turning the plateau blue in spring. It passes through Saint-Aubin-sur-Mer, home to a private 18th-century ch\u00e2teau (not open to visitors) that has been a listed historic monument since 1976, before crossing more fields to reach, a small resort village at the mouth of the river Sa\u00e2ne. Quiberville boomed as a seaside destination in the 19th century, and its pebble beach, backed by tall chalk cliffs and lined with old bathing villas, still marks a natural stopping point on the GR 21 before the trail turns toward Dieppe.\nFrom Quiberville the trail climbs past the Phare d'Ailly lighthouse and through the woods of Vasterival and the Bois des Moutiers before reaching Varengeville-sur-Mer, a village of grand villas overlooking the sea. Its 12th-century church, rebuilt in the 16th century, is ringed by a cliffside cemetery with sweeping views over the Alabaster Coast; painter Georges Braque is buried here beneath a mosaic of his own design, and the church holds stained glass windows he made. From Varengeville the path descends through Sainte-Marguerite-sur-Mer and drops to the beach at Pourville-sur-Mer, then climbs a final time through farmland and woods above the cliffs before arriving in \u2014 brick architecture, harbours, and many seafood restaurants \u2014 a historic Channel port city that makes a fitting end point for this stretch of the GR 21.\nLeaving Dieppe, the trail passes below the Chapelle Notre-Dame-de-Bon-Secours and drops into Puys, a small clifftop resort where the novelist Alexandre Dumas p\u00e8re died. From there it climbs onto the Camp de C\u00e9sar, a fortified Gallo-Roman-era hilltop enclosure covering some 50 hectares above Bracquemont, before continuing north through a string of small resorts and valleuses \u2014 Belleville-sur-Mer, Berneval-le-Grand, and Saint-Martin-en-Campagne \u2014 with long, open stretches of farmland between them. The route then swings inland for a roughly 5-km detour around the Penly nuclear power station before rejoining the cliffs for one of the wilder, quieter sections of the whole GR 21, crossing the valleuses of Parfondval and Biville with few villages, shops, or places to eat along the way. The trail finally descends into, a small town at the mouth of the river Y\u00e8res whose pebble beach and pastel-coloured beach huts, along with those of its hamlet Mesnil-Val, mark the end of this stage; its chalk cliffs rise to around 107 m.\nThe final stretch of the GR 21 crosses the D22 over the river Y\u00e8res and climbs gradually back onto the cliffs, passing through the Plaine du Mesnil and the valleuses of Parfondval and Biville \u2014 a wild, sparsely populated finish to the trail with fine views back along the coast. The path skirts the hamlet of Mesnil-Val and its pebble beach, likely the last beach walkers will see before the end of the route, before making a final climb along the clifftop above \u2014 cliffs, fishing port, and seaside resort \u2014 the trail's official terminus. From the belvedere above town, hikers descend a flight of 365 steps (or take the funicular) down to the quays, arriving among Le Tr\u00e9port's brick townhouses, fishing boats, and seafront casino at the mouth of the Bresle.\nStay safe.\nKeep well away from the cliff edges \u2014 the chalk crumbles unpredictably, and the drop is fatal. Stick to the marked path, especially after rain or frost when the ground is at its most unstable. If a section is closed or diverted, follow the signs rather than pushing through."}
{"id": "168987", "revid": "2428195", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168987", "title": "GR 223", "text": "The GR 223 is a French hiking trail in the Grande Randonn\u00e9e network.\nUnderstand.\nIt is known as \"Sentier des Douaniers\" (\"Custom Officers' Way\"); the route is mostly coastal.\nPrepare.\nNo permits are required. The trail is marked throughout with red-and-white GR blazes, and apps like Komoot or Visorando can help. The route is mostly flat coastal walking with occasional cliff climbs \u2014 sturdy waterproof boots are the main equipment need. The path is not suitable for wheelchairs or prams due to beaches, rocky stretches, and uneven surfaces. Luggage transfer and hiker shuttles are available along parts of the route through services such as La Malle Postale.\nEat.\nTowns and villages at the end of each stage reliably offer restaurants, bakeries, and small shops, so there is no need to carry a stove or significant food reserves. The exception is the wilder stretches around Cap de la Hague and the Cotentin marshes, where services can be sparse \u2014 carry extra snacks on those days.\nDrink.\nFrench tap water is safe to drink and available at accommodation, caf\u00e9s, and town fountains throughout the route. Carry 1\u20131.5 litres between settlements on quieter sections. Avoid drinking from streams and burns untreated, as agricultural run-off is common along the coast.\nSleep.\nHotels, B&amp;Bs, g\u00eetes d'\u00e9tape, and campsites are found at the end of most stages. A sleeping bag is unnecessary if staying in g\u00eetes or hotels, though a lightweight sheet liner is handy at simpler g\u00eetes. Book ahead in summer, as accommodation fills quickly between June and August. Wild camping is not legally permitted in France without landowner consent.\nClimate.\nSpring and autumn offer the most pleasant conditions. Normandy is temperate but wet and windy throughout the year, so waterproofs are essential regardless of season. The trail can be done in winter, though days are short, some accommodation closes, and coastal paths become muddy. Take extra care near beaches and estuaries during heavy rain and high tides.\nWalk.\nThe GR 223 follows the Normandy coast from Honfleur to Mont Saint-Michel, crossing the coast of Calvados and the Cotentin peninsula before reaching the Breton border. The full walk takes about a month, but many walkers choose shorter sections according to their interests: seaside resorts, D-Day history, wild Cotentin headlands, bird-rich estuaries, or the final approach to the Bay of Mont Saint-Michel.\nThe walk begins in, one of Normandy\u2019s most atmospheric harbour towns. From the old port, the route heads west along the Seine estuary and onto the, literally the \u201cFlowery Coast\u201d. This first stage is a gentle introduction to the GR 223, with sea views, wooded slopes, villas, and changing glimpses across the water before reaching the small coastal village of Villerville.\nThis section continues along the C\u00f4te Fleurie through some of Normandy\u2019s best-known Belle \u00c9poque resort scenery. The route passes near or through and, known for grand hotels, seaside promenades, beaches, and villas. West of Deauville, the coast becomes quieter again, leading toward Villers-sur-Mer and the cliffs around the Vaches Noires.\nLeaving Villers-sur-Mer, the trail follows the transition from resort coast to the chalk and clay cliffs of the Pays d\u2019Auge. This is one of the longer early stages, with wide beaches, exposed cliff-top views, and several small seaside settlements. The route approaches the eastern edge of the, the \u201cMother of Pearl Coast\u201d, as it moves toward the area around the Orne estuary.\nThis stage enters the D-Day landing coast. The route passes close to Ouistreham and Sword Beach before continuing west along the C\u00f4te de Nacre. The walking is generally flatter and more open than on the earlier cliff stages, with long beaches, seaside towns, memorials, and remains of wartime defences. lies near the Juno Beach sector, making this a historically important part of the GR 223.\nA long stage through the heart of the D-Day beaches. From the Juno Beach area, the route continues toward Gold Beach and Arromanches-les-Bains, where remains of the Mulberry harbour can still be seen offshore. Farther west, the coastline becomes more varied, with cliffs, beaches, villages, and military cemeteries. The stage ends at, a fishing harbour between the Gold and Omaha Beach sectors.\nWest of Port-en-Bessin, the route reaches the dramatic Omaha Beach sector. This part of the walk combines open coastal scenery with some of the most important Second World War sites in Normandy. The trail passes near bluffs, beaches, memorials, and military cemeteries before moving inland and along quieter rural paths toward.\nThe landscape softens as the route approaches the lowlands between Calvados and the Cotentin. The trail moves through countryside, marsh edges, and small settlements before reaching, known for dairy produce and its position near the Vire estuary. This section marks the gradual transition from the D-Day beaches toward the wetlands of the Cotentin and Bessin.\nThis shorter stage leads into, at the edge of the Cotentin marshlands. The route passes near the, a broad wetland landscape of canals, meadows, birds, and seasonal flooding. Carentan is a natural stopping point between the Calvados coast and the Tour du Cotentin.\nFrom Carentan, the GR 223 heads toward the eastern side of the Cotentin peninsula and the Utah Beach area. This is another major D-Day section, combining marshland, military history, and open coastal walking. A detour inland allows walkers to visit, one of the best-known towns associated with the airborne landings.\nThe route returns toward the sea and follows the quieter eastern Cotentin coast. Compared with the beaches farther east, this section feels more rural and less built up, with dunes, low cliffs, small villages, and sea views across the Baie de Seine. It leads toward and the beginning of the Val de Saire.\nThis stage enters the Val de Saire, a fertile coastal area of villages, harbours, and open sea views. The route reaches, a handsome harbour town known for its Vauban fortifications. The nearby towers at Saint-Vaast-la-Hougue and Tatihou are part of the UNESCO-listed Vauban defences.\nA relatively short and attractive coastal stage leads north from Saint-Vaast-la-Hougue to. The walking follows the eastern tip of the Cotentin past harbours, stone houses, beaches, and views toward Tatihou and the Channel. Barfleur is one of the most charming fishing villages on the route, with granite quays and a compact historic centre.\nFrom Barfleur, the trail continues around the north-eastern corner of the Cotentin. The Phare de Gatteville is one of the major landmarks of this stage, standing above a rocky and exposed coastline. The route then follows a more rugged shore toward, with changing views, small coves, and a stronger sense of the open Channel.\nThe GR 223 turns west toward. This section mixes natural coastline with the approaches to one of Normandy\u2019s major port cities. The route passes forts, harbours, beaches, and suburban coastal paths before reaching Cherbourg-en-Cotentin, a useful resupply and transport point on the route.\nWest of Cherbourg, the scenery becomes wilder as the trail enters La Hague. The coast rises into cliffs, headlands, heathland, and dry-stone-walled fields. This is the beginning of one of the most scenic parts of the GR 223, often nicknamed \u201cLittle Ireland\u201d for its rough green landscape and Atlantic feel. The stage ends around, a small village tucked into the Hague coast.\nThis is one of the classic nature stages of the route. The trail follows the wild coast around, west of Cherbourg, with cliffs, coves, moorland, and far-reaching sea views. Around Nez de Jobourg and Nez des Voidries, the cliffs are among the most dramatic on the Normandy coast.\nThe route begins to turn south along the western side of the Cotentin. The stage remains rugged and exposed, with cliff paths, heathland, and views over the Channel. The area around is known for dunes and open coastal landscapes, marking the transition from the high cliffs of La Hague to the long beaches and dunes of the C\u00f4te des Isles.\nThis stage follows the C\u00f4te des Isles southward. The coastline opens into longer beaches backed by dunes, with occasional rocky headlands and wide views. is a beach settlement below the hills of the western Cotentin, and the walking here offers a strong contrast with the tighter coves and cliffs of La Hague.\nThe route continues south through one of the more natural stretches of the western Cotentin coast. Beaches, dunes, and heathland dominate the scenery, with broad views over the sea and toward the Channel Islands in clear weather. The stage leads toward, close to large dune systems and the Barneville-Carteret area.\nThis section passes near, one of the main resorts on the C\u00f4te des Isles. The route then follows the coast south toward, with beaches, dunes, tidal inlets, and harbour scenery. The havres, or coastal estuaries, become increasingly important features of the landscape from here southward.\nThe GR 223 continues along a coast shaped by tides, dunes, salt marshes, and havres rich in wildlife. This is quieter walking, with fewer large towns and a strong sense of open space. makes a convenient coastal stop beside one of the characteristic estuaries of the western Cotentin.\nThis stage continues south past beaches, dunes, and low-lying coastal marshes. The route passes through a landscape where the sea, sand, and tidal channels constantly shape the walking environment. is associated with both coastal scenery and inland heritage, including the well-known Ch\u00e2teau de Pirou a short distance from the shore.\nThe walk carries on along the western Manche coast toward. This is a seaside stage of broad beaches, holiday settlements, dunes, and open horizons. The terrain is generally easier than the cliff sections farther north, though wind and tide can strongly affect the character of the walk.\nFrom Agon-Coutainville, the GR 223 approaches the havre of Regn\u00e9ville, one of the notable estuary landscapes of the western coast. The section combines beach walking, marsh edges, and views across tidal flats. is an attractive historic village with harbour scenery and remnants of its medieval castle.\nThis stage heads south toward the Granville area, continuing through the sequence of beaches, dunes, and tidal inlets. The walking remains open and maritime, with big skies and views over the western Normandy coast. is a beach settlement north of Granville and a useful stopping point before the route reaches the old corsair town.\nThe GR 223 passes near, an old corsair town perched on its rock. Granville is one of the major highlights of the southern part of the route, with harbour views, an old upper town, and links to the Chausey Islands. South of Granville, the trail begins to approach the Bay of Mont Saint-Michel.\nThis stage enters the landscape of the. The route passes the dramatic Champeaux cliffs, which offer some of the first sweeping views toward the silhouette of Mont Saint-Michel. The walking is increasingly shaped by the bay: tidal flats, salt meadows, distant sandbanks, and wide horizons.\nAt, walkers may be able to join a guided low-tide crossing of the bay, but this should only be done with proper local guidance because of tides, mud, and quicksand. The GR 223 itself continues around the bay, giving changing views of Mont Saint-Michel across the tidal flats as it heads toward Pontaubault.\nThe final stage follows the last approach to. The abbey and village dominate the horizon as the route crosses the edge of the bay and reaches one of France\u2019s most famous landmarks. Here the GR 223 ends and meets the GR 34, completing the coastal crossing of Normandy.\nStay safe.\nWord has it that there are problems with cliff erosion over certain sections, so don't be surprised if you are diverted inland at some points."}
{"id": "168989", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168989", "title": "Quezon City/Cubao", "text": "Cubao is the commercial heart of Quezon City, at the intersection of EDSA and Aurora Boulevard. While long overshadowed by North EDSA with its large malls, the districts's status as the traditional shopping center of Quezon City refuses to die; the Araneta family, who owns Araneta City, has begun a redevelopment project for the area to return it to its former glory.\nUnderstand.\nWhile the Triangle and Scout Area is the government, entertainment and nightlife hub, Cubao is the grand old commercial heart of Quezon City, with Araneta City[dead link], formerly Araneta Center, at its core. This district only developed in the late 1950s, when the Araneta family bought a piece of rural land once owned by RCA and built its first buildings. Cubao started to blossom with the construction of the Araneta Coliseum, which further become famous for the \"Thrilla in Manila\" boxing match between Muhammad Ali and Joe Frazier in 1975. The first shopping mall to open is Ali Mall, named after Muhammad Ali; other buildings followed, like SM Cubao, New Frontier Theatre (Kia Theatre from 2015 until 2018), Fiesta Carnival (formerly a Shopwise supermarket), and Farmers Plaza. The popularity of Cubao slowly declined as larger shopping malls and commercial developments begin to appear outside the area.\nCubao is not limited to Araneta Center, administered as part of barangay Socorro; the district extends into barangays Immaculate Conception, Kaunlaran, San Martin de Porres (Arayat), San Roque, and Silangan, all part of the former barangay until it was divided into smaller barangays. Local addresses continue to utilize Cubao as the barangay.\nGet in.\nBy public transit.\nCubao lies at the intersection of LRT Line 2 (LRT-2) and MRT Line 3 (MRT-3) and is reachable from both of them. The stations of both lines are named Araneta Center-Cubao, though these do not have any physical connection and transferring between the lines would involve walking on local streets. LRT-2 runs above Aurora Avenue west from Manila into Santolan in Pasig. MRT-3 is built at the middle of EDSA and runs from Taft Avenue in Pasay to North Avenue within Quezon City.\nBy bus.\nBy city bus.\nCubao is served by three bus routes as of August 2022, which is EDSA Carousel, 3 Antipolo\u2013Quiapo, 51 VGC-Cubao via Mindanao Avenue and 53 Cubao-Pacita. Route 51 and 53 buses terminate at Araneta Center, EDSA Carousel buses stop near P. Tuazon Boulevard, and route 3 buses stop along Aurora Boulevard \nBy provincial bus.\nDespite having a centralized bus station, many companies have established their own terminals elsewhere near Araneta Center; most around EDSA. This is going to change however, and those will be closed pending a court order.\nFor services from South Luzon and interisland trips, there are:\nFrom the north, there are:\nFor trips roughly within 200 km or 2 hours long, there are often no frills, but there will be vendors entering the bus at certain points and selling food and drinks at inflated prices. Longer trips will tend to have meals included on the fare, tickets must be purchased at a counter at the terminal, and on overnight trips and interisland services, there will be complimentary blankets and a ferry and port surcharge, respectively.\nBy airport bus.\nFrom Ninoy Aquino International Airport, you can take the Cubao route of UBE Express[dead link] to Araneta City Busport, per head. Travel time can over 30 minutes to 1 hour depending on traffic on EDSA.\nBy jeepney.\nThere are jeepneys to Cubao and from Manila and Antipolo, but as with buses, is hardly suggested due to the traffic. If traveling from the east, beware of \"patok\" jeepneys, which play fast and loud music and are driven erratically (often when requested by adventurous youth), but you'll hardly find them since police have cracked down on those.\nBuy.\nWhile it lost its status as the major shopping center in Quezon City over other larger malls built elsewhere, Cubao still retains its malls, supermarkets, and public market, while already dated, still gives the impression of the district's former glory.\nDrink.\nCoffee.\nThe area has Starbucks branches at Ali Mall, Gateway 2 and Araneta Coliseum, a outside Fiesta Carnival, a Bo's Coffee inside SM Cubao, a Coffee Bean and Tea Leaf branch outside Gateway 1, and a Tim Hortons at the New Frontier Theatre, among others.\nSleep.\nSplurge.\nAs of 2018, only Novotel is the splurge hotel around Cubao; the second splurge hotel to open is the 24-storey Ibis Styles Hotel, the first hotel in the Philippines under the Ibis brand; construction begun in 2016.\nConnect.\nGateway 1 and 2 has a limited free Wi-Fi service for Globe and Smart subscribers, although it is generally unreliable. Farmer's Plaza also has some internet cafes, although most have since closed down."}
{"id": "168993", "revid": "2357871", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168993", "title": "Soe", "text": "Soe is a city in West Timor.\nUnderstand.\nSoe is the capital of the South Central Timor regency. Due to its pronunciation (so-E), sometime it is written as \"SoE\". It is a highland area with an altitude of 800m, so the climate is cooler than the surrounding coastal Timor area. Travelers use this town is the base for day trips to explore the area. It is also possible to stay in the smaller towns around Soe nearby the villages. \nIf you intend to visit the traditional villages, it is recommended to get a local guide, as there are numerous customs to follow and language remains a barrier (even if you speak Bahasa Indonesia). The most common customs is that you need to be introduced to the village chief and bring betel nuts as gifts. A day trip usually costs Rp1,000,000 (as of Dec 2018). Some guides will give you an all-inclusive rate (incl. donation to the village for entrance), but it is recommended to give the donation directly to the village chief (instead of through the guide) to make sure the village received the full intended amount. The donation and betel nuts should sum up to around Rp100,000 (as of Dec 2018). You can purchase betel fruits / nuts around town. \nGet in.\nFrom Kupang: 3 hours. Trip started from Oesapa \"bus stop\" (it's a street side), where you can find \"Travel\" shared car for Rp50,000 or public bus for Rp30,000 (Bus to Kefamenanu will pass by Soe too, ask the bus assistant). Ask your accommodations for details (as of Dec 2018). If you are taking the bus, you can alight along the main street of Soe (Jln. Ahmad Yani or Jln. Gajah Mada). \nGet around.\n\"Bemo\" is running through the city, just tell your destination to the assistant. A ride per person costs Rp3,000 (as of Dec 2018). Many of the drivers and assistants are teenagers (don't be surprised).\n\"Ojek\" is on the standby on the road side. A ride within town should be Rp5,000-10,000 (as of Dec 2018). \nSee.\nYou can check with the Soe Tourist Office at Jln. Dipenogoro (closed on Saturday and Sunday) to get information on the local sights and contacts to tour guides. There are many tour / travel offices in the area, but they are mainly serving airline booking. \nEat.\nThere are many eating options on the main street of Soe, mostly offers \"bakso\" (great for the chilly night).\nGo next.\nAs other towns, you can wait on the side of the town's main road, wave on the bus and hop on to it."}
{"id": "168994", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=168994", "title": "Quezon City/Diliman and Katipunan", "text": "Diliman and Katipunan Avenue are the two university towns of Quezon City, housing two major rivalling universities, the University of the Philippines Diliman and the Ateneo de Manila University.\nUnderstand.\nDiliman, named after the Tagalog name for \"Stenochlaena palustris\", a species of medicinal fern, formed parts of the towns of Marikina and San Juan, then parts of Rizal province; when Quezon City is created, their governments supported the handover of those areas as part of the new capital of the Philippines that time. Formerly one barangay, Diliman has been since split up into eleven barangays, but its name survives in many addresses around the area, and is a \"de facto\" district.\nGet in.\nBy bus or jeepney.\nCommonwealth Avenue, which runs through this area, is served by numerous bus routes; buses can take you here from most corners of Metro Manila. Major bus and jeepney stops are Philcoa (nearest to Maguinhawa Street), UP Technohub (nearest to University of the Philippines Diliman), Central Avenue, Tandang Sora, Luzon Avenue, Ever Gotesco, Batasan Road, and Litex. If you take a bus, the conductor will call out the next stop.\nBy train.\nDiliman will be served by the future MRT Line 7, with one new station, University of the Philippines, at the middle of Commonwealth Avenue in front of Ayala Technohub. Katipunan, meanwhile, is served by LRT Line 2, with Katipunan station at the corner of Katipunan and Aurora Avenues.\nBy car.\nDiliman is served by two major thoroughfares:\nEat.\nThe Diliman area hosts Maginhawa Street, a four-lane street through the residential areas near UP Diliman turned into a food-tripping hub. Not only Maginhawa is the food hub; other major streets in Diliman have rows of restaurants within meters apart.\nKatipunan Avenue.\nJT's Manukan Grille Katipunan (G.Park)\n149 B. Gonzales St. 0963 048 9974\nJust Located Near Ateneo and Miriam College, this popular Inasalan (grilled chicken) is owned by popular actor Joel Torre and his wife, Cristy. It all began when the couple started experimenting with various marinades and recipes passed down from their parents and aunts. And while the desire to share their delicacy with others burned fiercely, turning it into a business was also inevitable.\nIt's not that busy all day even though it's in the vicinity of two schools so you can easily find seating. it is recommended to use both of the sauces to enhance your dining experience"}
