{"id": "169921", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=169921", "title": "Wukro", "text": "Wukro is a town in the Tigray region of northern Ethiopia. It can be used as a base for exploring the rock-hewn churches of north-eastern Tigray.\nUnderstand.\nWukro is a town in the Tigray Region of northern Ethiopia. It lies along the main road between Mekele and Adwa and is known for its historic rock-hewn churches and the surrounding highland landscape.\nWukro is located in a dry highland environment characterised by rocky hills, valleys and agricultural land. Farming and local trade are important to the surrounding communities.\nThe town is particularly notable for its connection to Tigray's ancient Christian heritage. Several churches in and around Wukro were carved directly into rock, reflecting the region's distinctive religious architecture.\nHistory.\nWukro and the surrounding area have a long history associated with the ancient civilisations of northern Ethiopia. The wider Tigray region contains archaeological remains dating back many centuries.\nThe town became an important settlement along the road connecting Mekele with the historic cities of Adwa and Axum.\nWukro and the wider Tigray region were affected by the Tigray conflict that began in 2020. Travellers should check the current security and access situation before visiting historic sites.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nBuses going between Adigrat and Mekele can drop passengers off at Wukro.\nGet around.\nThe town is small and walkable.\nWalking, taxis and local minibuses are the main ways to travel around Wukro. Private vehicles are useful for visiting attractions outside the town."}
{"id": "169922", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=169922", "title": "Devils Marbles Conservation Reserve", "text": "Devils Marbles (also known by the Aboriginal name Karlu Karlu) is a reserve protecting a natural landmark in the Barkly Tableland region of the Northern Territory, located just off the Stuart Highway. With an area of 18.02 km\u00b2 and established in 1961, it is located just 100km south of Tennant Creek.\nUnderstand.\nDevils Marbles are large granitic boulders precariously balanced on top of one another, which glow fiery red as sunset hits. They make for a nice stopover on the long Stuart Highway, and are culturally significant to the traditional Aboriginal owners, the Warumungu, who believe that these are the eggs of the Rainbow Serpent \u2013 a creature of a Dreamtime story.\nLandscape.\nThe 'marbles' themselves are the main landscape feature. The surrounding is mostly flat desert.\nClimate.\nThe area is a desert. In the summer, temperatures get very high, while nights can get very cold.\nGet in.\nEasy to access by car on the Stuart Highway, and it's the only way to get into the reserve and is about 100km south of Tennant Creek. No buses, trains or whatever.\nGet around.\nThe area is not big, and can be walked.\nEat.\nNo places to eat. You'll need to bring your own food.\nStay safe.\nThis area is hot and dry - drink a lot of water."}
{"id": "169935", "revid": "195012", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=169935", "title": "Philadelphia International Airport", "text": "Philadelphia International Airport, (), \u260f is the main airport serving Philadelphia, Pennsylvania and the surrounding Delaware Valley. It is the largest commercial airport in the state of Pennsylvania.\nUnderstand.\nThe site was first used as a training field by the Pennsylvania National Guard in 1925 before it was dedicated by Charles Lindbergh as the Philadelphia Municipal Airport in 1927. A terminal building was built in 1940, marking the beginning of commercial air service at the airport. The airport was used as a First Air Force training field during World War II. The airport became Philadelphia International Airport in 1945 and international flights began serving the airport. The airport has undergone numerous expansion projects as air travel has grown over the years.\nFlights.\nThe airport has seven terminals: A-West, A-East, B, C, D, E, and F. Terminal A-West is used for international flights while the remaining terminals primarily serve domestic flights. American Airlines operates a hub at the airport and utilizes multiple terminals, including exclusive use of three terminals.\nNote 1: Check your departure or arrival terminal if flying American Airlines. International and Caribbean flights use Terminal A-West, Domestic flights use Terminals A-East, B, and C, and American Eagle regional flights use Terminal F.\nNote 2: International flights for Frontier Airlines arrive at Terminal A-East\nNote 3: American Airlines \"flights\" to/from Allentown, Atlantic City, Lancaster, Trenton, Willkes/Barre (Scranton), and Wilmington are operated by Landline as a bus from behind security (airside). They are for travelers transferring between those other airports and American's actual flights in Philadelphia then by bus only to Philadelphia as a final destination or between these cities such as from Wilkes/Barre to Wilmington via PHL. Fares are included with the airfare for flying further, but are much more expensive for bus only with American Airlines/Landline than with SEPTA, NJ Transit, Greyhound, Martz, Uptown Vans, etc.\nGround transportation.\nBy car.\n PHL is right off Interstate 95 southwest of Center City Philadelphia, off exit 12 northbound and exit 12A southbound. Interstate 95 northbound leads toward Philadelphia and bridges over the Delaware River into the state of New Jersey, while Interstate 95 southbound leads toward the western suburbs in Delaware County and the state of Delaware.\nParking garages on-site allow for parking at PHL. Short-term parking is available in the ground level of the garage adjacent to terminals A-F. The rate for short-term parking is $4 for up to 30 minutes, $6 for up to 1 hour, $8 for up to 1.5 hours, $10 for up to 2 hours, $12 for up to 2.5 hours, $15 for up to 3 hours, $17 for up to 3.5 hours, $24 for up to 4 hours, and $44 for up to 24 hours. The rate for long-term parking the upper levels of the parking garage is $4 for up to 30 minutes, $6 for up to 1 hour, $8 for up to 1.5 hours, $10 for up to 2 hours, $12 for up to 2.5 hours, $15 for up to 3 hours, $17 for up to 3.5 hours, $24 for up to 24 hours. Long-term parking in the Economy Parking lot has an $11 daily flat rate for parking up to 24 hours, and a free shuttle is provided to and from the terminals. Several private parking lots adjacent to the airport also offer paid long-term parking, with shuttle service to and from the airport. \nA free cell phone parking lot is available for people waiting to pick up someone at the airport.\nRental cars are available at PHL, with each rental car agency providing free shuttles between the baggage claim and their facility. Rental car agencies serving PHL include Alamo, Avis, Budget, Dollar, Enterprise, Hertz, and National.\nBy taxi.\nTaxi service to/from PHL is provided by multiple companies. Taxis pick up and drop off along the commercial vehicle road adjacent to the terminal. A flat rate of $28.50 one-way is charged for taxi rides to Center City Philadelphia, with an additional $1 per passenger over 12 years old (up to $3). Taxi rides to points outside Center City Philadelphia are metered, with a base rate of $2.70 plus $2.30 per mile; the minimum fare is $10. All taxis serving PHL accept credit cards. Taxi companies[dead link] serving PHL include All City Cab, Capital, City Cab, Crescent, Liberty, Olde City, Philadelphia Coach, PHL Cab, \nQuaker City, Victory, and Yellow Cab.\nBy limousine and van.\nLimousine and van service to/from PHL is provided by multiple private companies. These services pick up and drop off along the commercial vehicle road adjacent to the terminal. Reservations for limousine and van services can be made at the Ground Transportation Information desk in the baggage claim. The desk will notify you when your service has arrived. Limousine and van service companies[dead link] serving PHL include AAA Airport One Direct, Call-First Transit, Dave's Best Limo, Delaware Express, Rapid Rover, and Tropiano.\nBy ride-hailing services.\nRide-hailing services Uber and Lyft provide service to/from PHL. Uber and Lyft pick-up is along the outer lanes of the arrivals road.\n* \n* \nEat and drink.\nPHL has a wide variety of dining options ranging from fast food to sit-down restaurants. The airport's Take Out for Take Off program allows food purchased in the airport terminal to be taken on-board the plane and eaten there. The airport has numerous fast-food and fast-casual chains including Auntie Anne's, Burger King, Chick-fil-A, Dunkin' Donuts, Sbarro, Starbucks, and Subway.\nSome of the more unique dining options at PHL include:\n*\n*\n*\nConnect.\nFree Wi-Fi is offered at PHL by AT&amp;T.\nSleep.\nThere are several other hotels near PHL in neighboring areas of Southwest Philadelphia and Tinicum Township. These accommodations vary from relatively inexpensive motels to upscale full-service hotels to extended stay properties. Many of these hotels offer free shuttle service to and from the airport.\nNearby.\nPhiladelphia is northeast of the airport, with Southwest Philadelphia and South Philadelphia particularly close to the airport. Tinicum Township in Delaware County is west of the airport."}
{"id": "169936", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=169936", "title": "National parks of Belarus", "text": "There are four national parks in Belarus. The Belavezhskaya Pushcha National Park is a trans-boundary park between Belarus and Poland. Together parks cover more than 3300 km2.\nPark entry fees.\nAll parks in Belarus are free. \nReservations.\nCampgrounds may be reserved in advance. Booking is possible on local websites.\nRespect.\nIn short: Leave-no-trace camping is always advised in national parks.\nDisturbing wildlife is illegal in a national park. Leave rocks, plants, bones and antlers as you found them. You may need to pack out any rubbish with you when you leave; if there are no latrines in a sensitive location, excrement should be packed out or buried. Anything left behind in the far north may take a very long time to decay, if it's biodegradable at all.\nMany parks are in remote or forested locations with essentially no local firefighting capability. A cook stove is preferable to an open camp fire, due to risk of wildfires. Keep any fires small enough to burn to ash before you leave. Never build a fire on moss or Arctic tundra where it can spread underground.\nDo not leave markers, messages or other manmade indicators behind; leave the parkland in its natural, untouched state for the next voyager. In some wilderness locations without marked permanent camp sites, leave-no-trace camping is advised."}
{"id": "169983", "revid": "2296660", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=169983", "title": "Beiji Village", "text": "Beiji Village is near Mohe in Daxing'anling Prefecture, Heilongjiang Province. The name Beiji itself means \"northernmost\" and accurately reflects its location in the northernmost part of China. Here you'll find the northernmost point in China, the northernmost outpost, and the northernmost post office, all of which are tourist attractions; there is also a Santa Claus House theme park about 25 km from Beiji Village (best experienced in the winter). The village itself is quite remote, nestled deep within the Greater Khingan Mountain Range along the Heilong River (Heilongjiang; also called the Amur River), and sparsely populated.\nUnderstand.\nThis area is quite remote and sparsely populated. In some parts, there isn't even a cellphone signal. The climate is subarctic with a January average low temperature of -36 degrees Celsius. Winter essentially lasts from mid-October to late-April. Summers are short but very warm given the latitude.\nGet in.\nAccess is by road through a forest preserve (as of January 2019, it cost 65 Renminbi to enter the preserve known as \"Arctic Village\", that's on top of the few hundred Renminbi you will have to pay for a taxi from Mohe Railway Station (China's northernmost railway station) or Mohe Gulian Airport (China's northernmost airport).\nThere are also a few daily public buses to and from Mohe Bus Station. Cost is \u00a525 one way (10/2018)\nGet around.\nMost of the area is quite remote; so, the only way to get around is to drive or arrange to have someone drive you.\nSee.\nMost everything \"northernmost\" in China is here - the northernmost point, northernmost outpost, northernmost village, and northernmost post office among them. As the village is on the Heilong River, you can look across and see Russia. The village is in a forest preserve and the forest itself is quite beautiful. Around the time of the Summer Solstice, you might be fortunate enough to see the Aurora Borealis.\nDo.\nThere's a place to ski about 25 kilometers from the village. There's a Santa Claus House theme park near the ski site. In the village itself, there's a square on the river's edge, a snowman valley, in addition to the aforementioned northernmost outpost and northernmost post office. There are signs in various parts of the village pointing to things to do.\nBuy.\nAs this region of China has a subarctic climate and discontinuous permafrost, there isn't much agriculture. However, blueberries grow wild here and there is quite an industry for blueberry products such as candies, jams, juices, and wines (including ice wine). You can buy post cards and souvenirs at the northernmost post office (and even have them mailed from there).\nEat.\nLocal restaurants are quite small, but serve typical regional dishes.\nDrink.\nThe blueberry wine is surprisingly good, particularly the ice wine.\nSleep.\nWithin Beiji Village there are lots of places to stay, mostly hostels and farm stays - you won't find any national or international hotel chains here."}
{"id": "169987", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=169987", "title": "Mohe", "text": "Mohe (\u6f20\u6cb3, M\u00f2h\u00e9), in the Daxing'anling region of Heilongjiang Province, is the northernmost county-level city in China and is often referred to as the \"Arctic of China\". Known for its unique geographical location, extreme cold climate, and distinctive natural and cultural attractions, Mohe has become a popular destination for tourists.\nUnderstand.\nMohe's northern edge is on the Heilong (Amur) River that borders Russia.\nThe climate is subarctic, with frigid winters and warm summers and discontinuous permafrost. Mohe, the northernmost city in China, attracts visitors with its unique \"northernmost\" culture, extreme cold experiences, and the chance to see the aurora. With stunning snow-covered landscapes, pristine forests, and clear starry skies, it is an ideal destination for sightseeing and photography. People visit Mohe not only to challenge its harsh climate but also to experience its distinctive blend of nature and culture.\nClimate.\nMohe has a cold temperate continental climate.\nThe climate here is characterized by long, cold winters and short, cool summers. Mohe is one of the counties with the lowest temperature in China. The annual average temperature is about -5.5\u2103, and the extreme minimum temperature has reached -53\u2103. Winters are very cold, with temperatures reaching dozens of degrees below zero. Although summer is warm, the temperature difference between day and night is large, and precipitation is concentrated in July. The spring and autumn seasons are short and windy in spring, while the temperature drops rapidly in autumn and is prone to early frost and freezing damage.\nHistory.\nMohe, located at the northernmost point of China, has a rich history, once home to the Sushen and Tungusic peoples. It became a key frontier region during the Qing Dynasty and thrived due to gold mining. Today, Mohe is renowned for its unique Arctic scenery and ethnic culture, making it a significant borderland tourist destination in China.\nGet in.\nThe best ways in are to fly or take a train.\nBy plane.\nHowever, direct flights may be limited and usually require a transfer from cities such as Harbin.\nBy train.\nThere are no direct intercity bus services from Harbin to Mohe. The two cities are approximately 1,200 kilometers apart, and the primary mode of public transportation is by train. \nBuses.\nMohe is the northernmost city in China. While the public transport system is relatively simple, there are several bus routes connecting the city center with surrounding areas.\nThe following is the bus schedule (Nov 2024):\nTips:\nGet around.\nThe scenic spots in Mohe are far apart and there is no convenient public transportation. Chartering a car, driving yourself or joining a tour group are the more ideal ways to travel. If you choose to drive yourself, you need to pay attention to road conditions and weather changes, especially in winter when the roads may become icy.\nIf you don't have your own car, you'll need to arrange for a car and driver.\nBuses to Beiji leave at 10:50 and 16:00 and cost \u00a525. (10/2018) You could also walk for short distances.\nIn addition, you can also choose to charter a car or join a tour group, which is more convenient and faster, especially in winter, when local public transportation may not be as frequent as chartered services.\nSee.\nWhat there is to see in Mohe is the various \"northernmosts\": the northernmost point, the northernmost outpost, the northernmost village, the northernmost post office, and so on. There is a large forest preserve in which is located an \"Arctic Village\" (Beiji Village). You'll also want to see Heilongjiang First Bayand Beihong Village. Around the time of the Summer Solstice, it's sometimes possible to see the Aurora Borealis in Beiji Village.\nDo.\nFestivals.\nHere are some special festivals in Mohe, reflecting its unique geographical location and natural features:\nThese festivals combine Mohe's natural resources and regional characteristics, enhancing the visitor experience and showcasing the unique cultural charm of Mohe.\nBuy.\nThese Mohe specialty foods can be purchased at local shopping malls or souvenir shops.\nThere's also a shop in Beiji Village where you can buy Russian-made products.\nEat.\nMohe is located in the northernmost part of China. Its unique geographical location and climatic conditions have given birth to a series of delicacies with local characteristics.\nThese delicacies not only showcase the local flavor of Mohe, but also reflect the local residents\u2019 eating habits and unique ways of processing ingredients. If you have the opportunity to visit Mohe, try these authentic specialties.\nThe following are recommended restaurants.\nDrink.\nBlueberry wine is produced here.\nSleep.\nThere are plenty of small hotels, youth hostels, and farm stays, particularly in the \"city\" by the airport or train station, as well as Beiji Village and Beihong Village. Beiji Village is your best option.\nRecommendation: \nStay safe.\nMake sure you bring warm clothing if you plan to be in Mohe, especially during the frigid winter, though temperatures are low throughout the year\u3002In addition, batteries drain quickly in cold weather, so bring extra batteries and power banks.\nConnect.\nCell phone coverage around Mohe may be poor, especially in remote areas. Make sure to inform family or friends of your trip before setting off, and stay in touch wherever there is signal."}
