{"id": "170120", "revid": "898440", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=170120", "title": "Tundra", "text": "The tundra is a biome between the coldest parts of the Arctic or Antarctic and the temperate climates found e.g. in Northern Europe and southern Canada. The characteristic feature of tundra is the permafrost\u2014in summer, only the surface will thaw, but below the surface, the ground is frozen. \nUnderstand.\nThis biome is known for its large, grassy plains where it never gets hot due to the latitude; it is to a large part beyond the Arctic Circle. Tundra regions are more remote than temperate grassland regions, and trees are scarce or absent.\nOn the right is a graph showing average temperatures (Fahrenheit) for Nome, Alaska. The dotted line that goes through the center of the graph marks freezing temperature, 32 F. Climates that fail to consistently pass freezing temperature in summer are classified as ice cap climates, and exist primarily in Greenland, the Arctic, and the Antarctic, although all of these regions have tundra climates as well. The difference is that most ice cap climates have permanent ice and snow cover, often hundreds of metres deep, while tundra climates are mostly ice-free (on the surface) in summer. Tundra climates are barely habitable, while ice cap climates are uninhabitable and typically only visited for scientific purposes.\nAt these latitudes you might see aurora borealis (or australis), and experience polar night or the midnight sun (above the polar circles).\nDestinations.\nTundra appears most commonly on latitudes between 55 and 75 degrees. In the northern hemisphere, most of this band is part of North America, Europe (above 70\u00b0) and Asia.\nIn the southern hemisphere, these latitudes are mostly covered by ocean. Tundra-like conditions appear in Patagonia, on islands in the Southern Ocean (subantarctic and antarctic islands) and the fringes of Antarctica itself.\nStay safe.\nRemoteness has its own risks, and means help is not easily available in emergencies. The cold weather, which dominates the region, makes this problem only worse. The tundra provides little shelter in case of severe weather. Make sure you plan your trips adequately, have appropriate clothing and gear, and have experienced company."}
{"id": "170121", "revid": "2169258", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=170121", "title": "Broomfield (Colorado)", "text": "\"For other places with the same name, see Broomfield (disambiguation).\"\nBroomfield is a combined city and county of about 77,000 in the Denver Metropolitan Area of the western U.S. state of Colorado. Broomfield lies 14 mi northwest of Denver and 12 mi southeast of Boulder. The only other combined city and county in Colorado is the City and County of Denver, the state capital."}
{"id": "170130", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=170130", "title": "Ikoma", "text": "Ikoma (\u751f\u99d2) is a city in Nara Prefecture.\nUnderstand.\nThough not frequented by foreign tourists, the city is home to Nara Institute of Science and Technology (NAIST), one of the best research institutions in Japan.\nTourist information site.\nThe city's tourism association has a multilingual guide site[dead link].\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nFrom Kansai International Airport (), take the Limousine Bus, get off at Gakuenmae Station, cost \u00a52150.\nBy train.\nFrom Kansai International Airport (), take the Nankai train towards Osaka-Namba Station. After that, transfer to Kintetsu Line and get off at Ikoma Station. The duration of the trip from Osaka-Namba is around 40 minutes."}
